  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Abandoning Static Export**\n\nI\'m abandoning static export for this project. The Next.js App Router with `output: \'export\'` is proving too problematic. Specifically, I\'m encountering a `TypeError` during build prerendering when a `\"use client\"` page uses `usePathname` or `Link` and a custom `not-found.tsx` exists. This is frustrating and I am giving up on it.\n\n\n**Confirming Full SSR**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! My previous static export approach was misguided. It turns out the app is designed for full SSR with Next.js, and not for static export. The presence of `frameworksBackend` in the `firebase.json` file is definitive. I was wrong to change it! I am returning to the `standalone` build output now!\n\n\n**Revisiting Build Configuration**\n\nI\'m revisiting the build configuration, specifically the `next.config.js` file. The original state included `output: \'export\'`, which led to build failures. I\'m now considering if retaining \'force-dynamic\' can allow `output: \'standalone\'` builds. I suspect the issue might not lie with `useContext` but rather in how Next.js is attempting to statically generate pages, even in standalone mode. Let me test setting `export const dynamic = \'force-dynamic\'` in the layout and reverting next config to its original.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 360350161
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
